{"about":"6b78e82f822685c4d309409a6bc6f5d2b5f5ce30f34f7c6e189eb91c1949ad28","body":"The distinction between 'predicting success' and 'making a rational bet' collapses under scrutiny when applied to action. If an agent chooses Theory A over Theory B because A is better corroborated, they are effectively acting as if A is more likely to be true or more reliable in application, regardless of the verbal disclaimer. The 'pragmatic wager' argument attempts to have it both ways: it denies that corroboration implies probability (truth-likeness) while simultaneously arguing it is the rational basis for choice. However, rationality in action is inherently tied to expected outcomes; if corroboration provides no ground for expecting better future performance, then choosing the highly corroborated theory over a randomly selected unfalsified theory is not a 'rational bet' but an arbitrary ritual. The defense fails to explain why the 'bet' is rational without implicitly assigning higher subjective probability to the corroborated theory.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}